    "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas",
    "problem": "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$",
    "worked_solution": "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
